From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Date:     Wed, 13 Apr 94 1:35:03 GMT
Subject: [#94-4-89] RAPTOR: A trojan horse of anarchocapitalism ?

Alexander Chislenko mentions a game with changing rules--I came up
with one a while ago. The initial rules are simple--in turn, each
player adds a rule which must obey itself and all the previous
rules from that game. Rules are written down--this game is hard
enough without being a memory challenge as well. You win by being
the last player who's able to make a rule meeting the two
conditions, though the more usual end to a game is that people
play until they're tired of it--figure on allowing two-three
hours for a game.

I call it Word Lemma--it's based on Lemma, a rule-proposing game
from Kadon Enterprises, 1227 Lorene Dr. Suite 16, Pasadena MD 21122.
410-437-2163.  Lemma has a mildly complicated board and pieces in
three colors. Each rule must require some sort of play with the
pieces and the board. Kadon mostly sells logic geometry games
and puzzles. 

There's another game (whose name I forget) which permits amendment
and abolition of old rules that was described some years ago
in _Scientific American_.

Nancy Lebovitz

