   The answer would seem to best be addressed by asking the question in an 
   operational way: how can I make money from writing uncopywrited software?

The same way the Grateful Dead make money from not enforcing the
intellectual property rights to their music.

The same way cel-phone service providers make money from giving away
cel-phones.

If the people using your free software are giving you money for other
things then that can pay for your staff of programmers (or fund your
personal hacking).

If Mosaic had been written by a profit making firm to sell something
over the net, and set it up so that it automatically conneted to their
server, then their profits would be directly related to the number of
people running their software and thus the freeness of their software.

Controlling the operation of people's computers is a really powerful
thing to be able to do, and eventually those firms that limit the
distribution of their software through legal mechanisms and by making
it a hassle to copy are going to be at a competitive disadvantage as
they monopolize themselves out of the market.

   --David

