I noticed in this discussion of "why respect property rights..." that
no-one has mentioned the most obvious reason...(of course perhaps they did
and I never saw the post....happens all the time ;-(...)
That reason being mutual respect of rights, i.e. I won't steal your
property if you won't steal mine...I will respect you if you respect me.
It seems that this would be a more powerful reason to establish a "code of
ethics", yet it is certainly subject to an escalation problem. Tit for
tat, so to speak.
The threat of mutual annihilation kept everybody more or less intact
during the cold war...though there were moments....
Maybe if it were couched in slightly different terms...."I won't interfere
in your life if you won't interfere in mine..."
...but then I guess you must define interference....(e.g. my factory in
Yugoslavia can't _possibly_ be interfering with your air in Ukraine...)

Hmmmmmm...definition of terms. Is there such a thing as "fuzzy"
definitions? Fuzzy logic works where logic fails......

Pondering....
