Dan says-

> I was originally trying to express that force alone is not a good
> basis for a mutual agreement on property rights. I view property
> rights, and rights in general, in operation as mutually enforced
> truces in a war for control. When you rely on your gun alone to do
> your convincing, you leave yourself open to being similarly convinced.

My model is that property starts from forceful defense, and then gets 
amplified a lot by social and other processes.  For instance, people in
a neighborhood being friends end up "joining forces," as they say.
Plus there are non-coercive things like shunning that can "enforce"
claims.  Eventually you get to a point where force rarely has to be
used...but I think the "effective defense" point of view is the best
way to see which kinds of arrangements will work out long-term.

> One must take into account the force that people can bring against you
> in performing the negotiations about rights, but I think it is a
> mistake for the side with the upper hand to rely on it. This only
> encourages the weaker side to improve its force projection potential,
> leaving both sides fighting the war instead of turning to productive
> pursuits, leaving both side in a negative sum game. Politics can e
> fruitfully analyzed in this manner.

The thing is, buying more guns doesn't change the effective balance of 
power much between you and somebody else in a social situation.  
Everybody else together still outgun you, and you're just going to make 
them madder.  So even though the whole thing's about force, it's not 
*brute* force that wins.

-fnerd
