Subject: [#94-4-102] land property

It's strange holding two simultaneous conversations on the same topic
between the same two people (Dan and I).  I said-

> > Also none of us seems to
> > have the energy for a complete essay on property rights.  

So Dan says-

> Ha ha, well yes, you noticed that too. It is not entirely just lack of
> energy on my part, however. Look at my position. You claim that those
> without land should respect the claims of the landed. If I come up
> with a theory on property rights, and show how it is not sufficient
> for your claim, you'll just say my theory is wrong, or at least
> incomplete. 

Yeah, but hopefully we'll differ in a way that's interesting to talk
about, not just differ in our conclusions.

> The bottom line place where I hope we can agree is that we should
> avoid using force against each other; that is it undesirable for us to
> get into situations in which we come into physical conflict.

Sure, although avoiding violence isn't a totally overriding concern.

>   You have
> proposed a solution in which some people have an advantage in the
> situation, and claim a right to use physical force against others,

? I think all property and all situations between people are 
asymetrical.

> while I have proposed one roughly symmetric. 

Er, do you mean symmetrical to my idea or to itself somehow?  I see
you as adding asymmetry to asymmetry--that people who have land get 
taxed by people who don't have land but do have other things the 
landed people don't.  I proposed a more symmetrical (to me) system
where everybody is taxed for everything they own, but I don't think
you've said much about that.

> The all other things
> being equal baseline would be a symmetric relationship, so I think you
> have the burden of proof.

Hmm.  How does "all other things being equal" apply to what you're
saying?  I love symmetry and "AOTBE", if you would align my sights
a little.

> Property as an institution of mutually enforced control makes for a
> useful concept to start with. You've given a description which suits
> me well enough. How about the prescription? When is it mutually
> advantageous, and roughly the best that either of us can expect to do
> in the negotiations, for us to agree with the other guy that he gets
> a certain control?

This is tricky for me to answer, because my main answer is, "property
happens."  Mainly the right thing to do is start with the status quo
and bargain with whatever chips you've got.  The main large-scale
change I'd make is to take away institutions that tax everyone and
enforce the same (albeit slanted) rules on everyone.  Instead let 
each person arrange and pay for the kind of defenses they want.

That gets you as far as "having a market," where we should draw a
dotted line "- - - - - - - - ".

Then, any suggestions below the line amount to business
proposals and self-help advice.  So, I guess, ask me again on the
next round.

